"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][27]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M27-L1 – Experiment Tracking and Data and Model Versioning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reconstruct and compare\nreproducible model runs using a versioned record of provenance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A model is more than a weights file<\/h2>\n<p>A workshop trains a model to predict spare-parts demand. Two people\neach save a file called <code>modell.bin<\/code>. Without knowing the\ntraining period, feature definitions and software environment, the files\nare difficult to interpret. Experiment tracking therefore records every\ntraining run as a separate event: code commit, data snapshot including\nits split, preprocessing, parameters, random state, environment, metrics\nand artefacts such as model weights or error plots. A model registry\nlinks selected artefacts to their review status, model ownership and\napproved versions. Experiment history and operational approval are\ndistinct activities.<\/p>\n<p>A version name is a human-readable reference; a cryptographic hash is\na fingerprint of particular bytes. A version must resolve to an\nimmutable reference. When personal data are involved, snapshots should\nbe stored under controlled access rather than copied into a public Git\nrepository. A hash proves neither lawful provenance nor scientific\nquality. It supports integrity checking, provided the expected hash\ncomes from a trustworthy source.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing two runs<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001568_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A shared test snapshot compares run A with MAE 1 and run B with MAE 0.333, retaining distinct artifacts.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A shared test snapshot compares run A\nwith MAE 1 and run B with MAE 0.333, retaining distinct\nartifacts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Provenance of two model runs.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Runs A and B are evaluated on identical synthetic test values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,6,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,7,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand B predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,5,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{MAE}=n^{-1}\\sum_i|y_i-\\hat y_i|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe respective errors are 1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSuch a comparison is meaningful only when selection procedures, test\ncases and transformations are consistent. In real model selection, a\nvalidation set is used; the held-out test set is reserved for final\nevaluation, not repeated hyperparameter selection. The diagram shows two\nruns with shared input references but separate result artefacts.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> hashlib, json<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>snapshot<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">b&#39;4,6,8<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>digest<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>hashlib.sha256(snapshot).hexdigest()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>runs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name,p <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>]),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>])]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    runs.append({<span class=\"st\">&#39;run&#39;<\/span>:name,<span class=\"st\">&#39;data_sha256&#39;<\/span>:digest,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                 <span class=\"st\">&#39;mae&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(y,p))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(y)})<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> runs[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>][<span class=\"st\">&#39;mae&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> runs[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"st\">&#39;mae&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> runs[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>][<span class=\"st\">&#39;data_sha256&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>runs[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"st\">&#39;data_sha256&#39;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(json.dumps(runs,indent<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducibility and approval<\/h2>\n<p>A seed alone cannot guarantee a bit-identical rerun: parallel\nreductions, library versions, hardware and non-deterministic operations\nmay affect the result. We should distinguish exact reproduction,\nstatistically similar reruns and scientifically independent replication.\nEvery published figure should be linked to its specific run. An alias\nsuch as <code>production<\/code> may point to a newly approved version,\nwhile the original version must remain identifiable. A rollback must\nconsider data, model and API compatibility. Development and approval\npermissions should be separated, and logs must not contain access\ncredentials.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A run with MAE 0.3 comes from a different test month than a run with\nMAE 0.4. Can it be selected on that basis alone? No. Differences in\ndemand or case difficulty may affect both figures. Both models should be\nevaluated on the same sample that was not previously used for selection;\nfor time-dependent data, document the temporal split.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/tracking/\">MLflow: ML\nExperiment Tracking<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning\">Google\nCloud: MLOps – Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine\nlearning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M27-L2 – Training Pipelines, Orchestration and CI/CD<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design dependencies in an ML\npipeline, validation gates and separate integration, delivery and\ntraining processes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a script to a directed workflow<\/h2>\n<p>A daily forecasting system must ingest new data, check types and\ntimestamps, generate features, train a model and evaluate the outcome.\nThese steps form a directed acyclic graph (DAG). An edge indicates that\na downstream step depends on an upstream result. Independent tasks may\nrun in parallel. An orchestrator manages state, dependencies, resources,\nretries and logs. Scheduling alone does not guarantee scientific\ncorrectness.<\/p>\n<p>Continuous Integration (CI) checks changes to code, tests and\nconfiguration. Continuous Delivery (CD) prepares a verified software or\nmodel artefact for release; fully automatic deployment is a separate\ndecision. Continuous Training (CT) initiates a fresh training run when\nspecified data, time or quality conditions are met. A newly trained\nmodel is initially a candidate, not automatically approved for\nproduction. A stable interface must also apply the same feature\ndefinitions at training and serving time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The critical path and approval gate<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001664_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Import and validation precede parallel feature preparation, model training and baseline evaluation, yielding a 27-minute critical path.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Import and validation precede parallel\nfeature preparation, model training and baseline evaluation, yielding a\n27-minute critical path.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Dependencies and critical path in a training\npipeline.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Example: data import takes 4 minutes and validation 2 minutes.\nFeature preparation then takes 5 minutes and baseline evaluation 3\nminutes, with the latter two running in parallel. Training takes 12\nminutes and depends on the features; final evaluation requires 4 minutes\nafter both training and baseline have finished. With no resource\nbottlenecks, the total is 27 minutes because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+2+\\max(5+12,3)+4=27<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRunning the tasks serially would take 30 minutes. The baseline is not on\nthe critical path. The diagram illustrates dependencies and durations.\nApproval is a separate subsequent step.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>jobs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{<span class=\"st\">&#39;Import&#39;<\/span>:(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,[]),<span class=\"st\">&#39;Validierung&#39;<\/span>:(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;Import&#39;<\/span>]),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>      <span class=\"st\">&#39;Merkmale&#39;<\/span>:(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;Validierung&#39;<\/span>]),<span class=\"st\">&#39;Baseline&#39;<\/span>:(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;Validierung&#39;<\/span>]),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>      <span class=\"st\">&#39;Training&#39;<\/span>:(<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;Merkmale&#39;<\/span>]),<span class=\"st\">&#39;Bewertung&#39;<\/span>:(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;Training&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Baseline&#39;<\/span>])}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>end<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name,(duration,deps) <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> jobs.items():<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    end[name]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>duration<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>((end[k] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> deps),default<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> end[<span class=\"st\">&#39;Bewertung&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">27<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(v[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> jobs.values())<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(end)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Handling failures and retries<\/h2>\n<p>A failed import must not silently cause old data to be reused.\nRetries must be controlled: an idempotent write produces the same\nlogical state when given the same input, for example by transactionally\ncommitting a result under a fixed run key. Cached results are reused\nonly with identical inputs, including code and parameters. Data\nvalidation covers value ranges, completeness and temporal plausibility\nas well as column types. Secrets belong in separate access-control\nfacilities. When error rates or model quality deteriorate, the\npreviously approved service may remain in use only if it is still safe\nfor its intended purpose; otherwise a defined fallback procedure is\nactivated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Baseline evaluation now takes 20 rather than 3 minutes. Total\nduration rises to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>17<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+2+\\max(17,20)+4=30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes. Making training faster would initially have no effect on\noverall duration, as the baseline is now on the critical path.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning\">Google\nCloud: MLOps – Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine\nlearning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/tracking/\">MLflow: ML\nExperiment Tracking<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M27-L3 – Hardware, Parallelism and Distributed Training<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish data, model and\npipeline parallelism and assess synchronisation and scaling\nnumerically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Memory and processing units<\/h2>\n<p>Training neural networks requires storage not only for weights but\nalso for activations, gradients and, where applicable, optimiser states.\nAccelerators have many processing units, but use them well only with\nsuitable parallel work and sufficient data supply. Data parallelism\nreplicates a model and distributes training examples across processes.\nGradients are synchronised after each local backward computation. Model\nparallelism distributes parameters or tensor operations because a model\ndoes not fit in one memory or can be computed more efficiently across\ndevices. Pipeline parallelism assigns layers to stages; several\nmicro-batches fill the pipeline, potentially creating idle time and\ncommunication overhead. Modern systems often combine these\napproaches.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gradient aggregation and speed<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001588_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Processes handle two and four examples; their weighted global mean is 8/3 rather than 2.75.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Processes handle two and four examples;\ntheir weighted global mean is 8/3 rather than 2.75.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gradients across two processes.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Process A handles two examples with gradients 2 and 4 for the same\nscalar parameter. Process B handles four examples with gradients 1, 1, 3\nand 5. Their local means are 3 and 2.5. The global mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2\\cdot3+4\\cdot2.5)/6=8/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot the unweighted mean of 2.75. When local batches have equal sizes,\naveraging their mean gradients yields the global mean. Unequal sizes\nrequire appropriately adjusted weights. The diagram shows both process\ngroups and their weighted reduction.<\/p>\n<p>For a fixed problem size with an ideally parallelisable fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nAmdahl’s model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(p)=1/[f+(1-f)/p]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand four processes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than 4, giving efficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>62.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S/p=62.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCommunication would reduce real speedup further.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a,b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>g<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>F(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(a)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(b),<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(a)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(b))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>wrong<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(F(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(a),<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(a))<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>F(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(b),<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(b)))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> g<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> wrong<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> s<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(g),<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(wrong),<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(s),<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(s<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Communication and practical scaling limits<\/h2>\n<p>All-reduce makes the aggregated result available to every\nparticipating process. Between machines, exchanging large gradients may\ndominate a training step. Doubling the global batch size also changes\noptimisation conditions: fewer updates per epoch, potentially a\ndifferent learning rate and different generalisation behaviour. Step\ntime, examples per second and time to reach a specified model quality\nare therefore distinct measures. Gradient accumulation sums\ncontributions from several micro-batches before an update, but needs\ncorrect weighting and affects techniques such as batch normalisation\ndifferently from one genuinely large batch. Mixed precision reduces some\nmemory and computing requirements, but calls for suitable accumulation\nprecision and numerical checks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand eight processes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>17<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.71<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=1/(0.1+0.9/8)=80/17\\approx4.71<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe theoretical limit is 10. Communication, memory bandwidth and uneven\nworkloads are excluded from this simplified model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/ddp_tutorial.html\">PyTorch:\nGetting Started with Distributed Data Parallel<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · tutorial\nconsulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/nmds/00-Background/02-Performance.html\">Bindel:\nNumerical Methods for Data Science – Performance Basics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching\ntext consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M27-L4 – Model Serving, Batching, Quantisation and Efficient Inference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish throughput, latency\nand quantisation error, and balance inference efficiency against quality\nconstraints.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How a model is served depends on its application<\/h2>\n<p>An hourly forecasting report can process many records together. An\ninteractive service must instead respond quickly to individual requests.\nServing includes serialisation, queues, preprocessing, routing and\noutput validation as well as model computation. Throughput measures\ncompleted requests per unit time; latency measures the time taken per\nrequest. Averages hide slow outliers, so median and 95th-percentile\nlatency, for example, are measured under representative load profiles.\nFor language models, time to first token and output token rate can be\nseparate objectives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Batch size and numerical representation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001519_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Batch sizes 1, 4 and 8 yield throughput 166.7, 333.3 and 400 requests per second at 6, 12 and 20 milliseconds.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Batch sizes 1, 4 and 8 yield throughput\n166.7, 333.3 and 400 requests per second at 6, 12 and 20\nmilliseconds.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Batch size, throughput and processing time.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a batch of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequests, a synthetic accelerator requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(b)=4+2b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms. At full utilisation and ignoring other overheads, it can process\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000b/t(b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequests per second: approximately 166.7 for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n333.3 for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 400 for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPure computation time per batch increases to 6, 12 and 20 ms\nrespectively. The diagram shows this trade-off. Waiting to form batches\nadds to latency; a maximum batch waiting time does not bound total\nend-to-end latency.<\/p>\n<p>Linear quantisation approximates real-valued numbers with integers:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">clip<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">round<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\operatorname{clip}(\\operatorname{round}(x/s)+z,q_{\\min},q_{\\max})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=s(q-z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an integer zero point. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.26<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.26<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaps to 3 and back to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe absolute error is 0.04. Without clipping, idealised rounding error\nis no more than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne million weights require 4 MB raw storage in FP32 or 1 MB in INT8\n(decimal units); scales, metadata and activations add to this.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> rate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>t<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.13<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.26<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.07<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.clip(np.rint(x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>),<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">128<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">127<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>xhat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(rate,[<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4000<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">400<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>xhat))<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(t,rate,q,xhat)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selecting a system under real constraints<\/h2>\n<p>Quantisation may save bandwidth and weight storage, but accelerates\ncomputation only with suitable hardware and runtime support. Calibration\ndata must cover relevant value ranges; quality is checked at task level\nand, when relevant, across subgroups. Dynamic batching collects\ncompatible concurrent requests until a capacity or time limit is\nreached. Stateful requests need suitable scheduling. Load tests measure\nthroughput, percentiles, timeout rates, memory and quality across\ndifferent loads and input lengths. An overloaded service requires\nbounded queues, rejection rules and possibly controlled fallbacks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A batch of four requests waits an additional 7 ms before starting. A\nrequest that was present at the start of that wait takes at least 19 ms\nto complete model computation, excluding transport and preprocessing. It\nalready misses a 15 ms latency target.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/triton-inference-server/user-guide/docs/user_guide/batcher.html\">NVIDIA\nTriton: Batchers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/performance/model-optimizations/quantization.html\">ONNX\nRuntime: Quantize ONNX models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M27-L5 – Monitoring, Drift and Retraining<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish changes in data\ndistributions from quality deterioration and justify a supervised\nretraining process.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What changes after deployment?<\/h2>\n<p>A model predicts whether a pump will fail in the next seven days. New\npump types may appear, sensors may be calibrated differently, or\nmaintenance rules may change failure probabilities. Covariate shift\nchanges\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile assuming stable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y\\mid X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLabel shift describes a change in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile assuming stable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X\\mid Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere, concept drift means a change in the conditional relationship\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y\\mid X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSeveral shifts may occur together in real data; diagnosis is often\nambiguous without observed outcomes.<\/p>\n<p>Monitoring covers system health (errors, latency, utilisation),\ninputs (schema, missing values and distributions), model behaviour\n(scores and predictions) and delayed task-specific quality measures. The\nactual outcomes for a seven-day forecast only become fully available\nlater. An input distribution change is therefore a warning sign, not\nproof of poor predictions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing two sensor distributions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001554_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Reference percentages 50, 30, 20 change to 20, 30, 50, giving total variation distance 0.30.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Reference percentages 50, 30, 20 change\nto 20, 30, 50, giving total variation distance 0.30.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Distribution shift in sensor measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Across three fixed temperature categories, reference proportions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=(0.5,0.3,0.2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand current proportions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(0.2,0.3,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe total variation distance between discrete distributions is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{TV}(p,q)=\\tfrac12\\sum_i|p_i-q_i|=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt lies between zero and one and depends on the category boundaries\nchosen. The figure shows the redistributed frequencies. An internal\nalert threshold of 0.2, specified in advance for this teaching example,\ninitiates an investigation but does not authorise a model release.\nSample size, random variation and seasonal changes also matter.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[F(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[F(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(p,q))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(d), <span class=\"st\">&#39;prüfen&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;weiter beobachten&#39;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From an alert to a decision<\/h2>\n<p>First check instrumentation, data provenance and timing. A faulty\nmeasurement station must not provide a training signal without\ninvestigation. Then examine quality metrics for representative subgroups\nusing fully observed labels. Evaluate a new candidate on historical data\nand an appropriate recent validation window, avoiding temporal leakage.\nIf suitability is demonstrated, use a controlled rollout. If evidence\nremains insufficient, document the unresolved decision or bridge the\nsituation with a safe fallback. Automated alerts require named owners\nand clear escalation paths; many simultaneous high-dimensional drift\ntests can produce numerous false positives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A new production batch gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(0.4,0.3,0.3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts distance to the original reference is 0.1, below the teaching\nthreshold. That does not prove that conditional failure probabilities\nare stable: a change in maintenance may degrade predictions even if\ntemperature categories remain unchanged.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11953\">Rabanser, Günnemann und\nLipton (2019): Failing Loudly: An Empirical Study of Methods for\nDetecting Dataset Shift<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning\">Google\nCloud: MLOps – Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine\nlearning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M27-L6 – Testing Strategies, Rollback and Operational Reliability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Connect ML-specific tests,\nservice objectives and staged rollouts to an error budget.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multiple levels of testing<\/h2>\n<p>A forecasting service can use correct model weights and still accept\nincorrect units. Unit tests check individual functions, such as\nconverting grams to kilograms. Integration tests examine the interaction\nof data source, feature processing, model and output. Contract tests\ncheck permissible input types and value ranges. Model tests include\nquality metrics, comparisons with a baseline, edge cases and appropriate\nsubgroups. Justified tolerances and repeated runs are useful for\nrandomised or numerically approximate systems. Load tests and failure\nsimulations assess operational performance, not just prediction\nquality.<\/p>\n<p>A Service Level Indicator (SLI) is a measured quantity; a Service\nLevel Objective (SLO) specifies a target for that quantity over a\ndefined window. An error budget quantifies the room between the target\nand perfect delivery, not permission to cause avoidable failures.\nCritical safety requirements may demand immediate action regardless of\nan aggregate SLO.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A limited rollout<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001535_en.svg\"\nalt=\"New version produces 100 failures and previous version 19; 119 total exceed the permitted budget of 100.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">New version produces 100 failures and\nprevious version 19; 119 total exceed the permitted budget of\n100.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Error budget during a limited rollout.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For 100,000 requests a day, a target of 99.9% successful responses\npermits at most 100 failures in that window. A new version initially\nreceives a randomly selected 5% of traffic and has a 2% error rate; the\nold version has a 0.02% error rate. Under exactly these teaching\nassumptions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5000\\cdot0.02=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>95000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.0002<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">95000\\cdot0.0002=19<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nerrors result. The total of 119, equivalent to 99.881% success, misses\nthe objective. The diagram compares error contributions and the budget.\nAn early canary alert could limit the impact. Traffic should be\nincreased only after sufficiently representative measurement.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100000<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> candidate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>new<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>candidate<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> old<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>candidate)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>budget<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> success<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(new<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>old)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> new<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> old<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">19<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> budget<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> success<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">999<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(new,old,budget,<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(success)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Failure handling and recovery<\/h2>\n<p>Shadow tests send copies of requests passively to a new version\nwithout returning its outputs to users; external write side effects must\nbe suppressed. By contrast, a canary receives live traffic. Rollback\nmust restore a known working model version, its preprocessing and\ncompatible interfaces. Data migrations can prevent rollback if older\napplications can no longer read the new format. Plan backward\ncompatibility, backups, tested restoration and safe abort criteria\nbefore rollout. Incident records should document chronology, impact,\ncauses and corrective measures, without unnecessarily logging sensitive\ninputs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Reducing the new version’s share to 1% under the same error rates\ngives an expected 20 errors from the new version and 19.8 from the old\nversion. The expected total stays below 100, although individual days\nmay differ. Serious task-specific errors must not be ignored even at low\ntraffic shares.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/\">Google SRE\nWorkbook: Canarying Releases<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 16 consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning\">Google\nCloud: MLOps – Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine\nlearning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M27-L7 – Costs, Energy Use and Technical Sustainability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate energy, costs and\noperational emissions with transparent system boundaries and evaluate\nefficiency measures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power is not the same as energy<\/h2>\n<p>A training task occupies an accelerator for several hours. Electrical\npower\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncombined with runtime determines the energy used:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\int P(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor constant average power, multiplying kilowatts by hours yields\nkilowatt-hours. The measurement boundary must be stated: an individual\nGPU, a whole machine, all IT infrastructure or a data centre. Power\nUsage Effectiveness (PUE) is the ratio of total data-centre energy to IT\nenergy within the same accounting boundary. If total energy is already\nmeasured, multiplying by PUE again would double-count overhead.<\/p>\n<p>Operational greenhouse-gas emissions also depend on location- and\ntime-appropriate electricity emissions intensity. Manufacturing,\ntransport and disposal of equipment are outside an\noperating-electricity-only calculation. Depending on how a system is\nrun, costs may include electricity, hardware depreciation, maintenance,\nnetworking or an agreed compute-hour tariff. A cloud hourly price often\nincludes a share of these underlying operating costs already.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two synthetic training options<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001724_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Variant A uses 5 kWh IT energy plus 1 kWh overhead; variant B uses 4 kWh plus 0.8 kWh.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Variant A uses 5 kWh IT energy plus 1 kWh\noverhead; variant B uses 4 kWh plus 0.8 kWh.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Energy use of two training variants.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Including CPU and memory, option A draws an average 0.5 kW of IT\npower for 10 hours and B draws 0.8 kW for 5 hours. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_A=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_B=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkWh. With a shared assumed PUE of 1.2, total energy is 6 and 4.8 kWh. At\nthe explicitly assumed electricity emissions intensity of 0.35 kg\nCO₂e/kWh, emissions are 2.1 and 1.68 kg CO₂e. With an electricity-only\nprice of €0.30/kWh, the energy costs are €1.80 and €1.44 respectively. B\nsaves 20% energy for the same assumed result quality. The figure\nseparates IT and overhead energy. These are not measurements from a real\ndata centre.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> decimal <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Decimal <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> D<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>it<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;.5&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;.8&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>total<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;1.2&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> it]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>co2<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;.35&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> total]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>price<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;.30&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> total]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> total<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>[D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;6&#39;<\/span>),D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;4.8&#39;<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> co2<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>[D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;2.1&#39;<\/span>),D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;1.68&#39;<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(it,total,co2,price)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measure efficiency against the correct objective<\/h2>\n<p>A model that meets the required quality using less compute may avoid\nunnecessary work. Yet shorter runtime does not always mean less energy:\npower and resource utilisation also change. Comparisons must use\nequivalent task quality, data and service boundaries, such as energy per\nthousand correct predictions at a specified latency objective. Discarded\nexperimental runs also count. Quantisation, reusing existing models,\nending unproductive searches early and choosing suitable hardware can\nhelp when testing confirms the quality. If demand rises rapidly, lower\nenergy per request can still coincide with higher total energy use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>B is now run in a location with electricity emissions intensity of\n0.55 kg CO₂e/kWh. At the same total energy use, it emits 2.64 kg CO₂e,\nmore than A under the original intensity. Energy efficiency and\noperational emissions must therefore be reported separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.09700\">Lacoste et al. (2019):\nQuantifying the Carbon Emissions of Machine Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract page\nconsulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/nmds/00-Background/02-Performance.html\">Bindel:\nNumerical Methods for Data Science – Performance Basics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching\ntext consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M27-L8 – High-Performance Data Analytics: Memory Hierarchy and Scaling Limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Optional specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate memory bandwidth and\narithmetic intensity and use the roofline bound to investigate\nperformance bottlenecks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why moving data is expensive<\/h2>\n<p>In numerical computing, a processor accesses several levels of\nmemory. Registers and nearby caches often provide quick access to small\namounts of data; main memory and external storage are larger but more\nconstrained per access or transferred byte. Many accelerators also incur\ntransfers between host and device. If a value is needed repeatedly, a\nsuitable data layout may enable reuse in cache. Contiguous accesses and\nblocked matrix operations can reduce unnecessary transfers under the\nright conditions. Very large arrays also encounter capacity limits, not\njust bandwidth limits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A theoretical performance bound<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001695_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Performance grows linearly up to arithmetic intensity 8 and remains capped at 400 GFLOP/s thereafter.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Performance grows linearly up to\narithmetic intensity 8 and remains capped at 400 GFLOP/s\nthereafter.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Roofline model with two performance limits.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Arithmetic intensity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=W/Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of floating-point operations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndivided by bytes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmoved across a specified memory boundary. With peak compute performance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{max}=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nGFLOP/s and memory bandwidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nGB/s (decimal), the roofline bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\le\\min(P_{max},BI)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFLOP/byte the bound is 100 GFLOP/s; at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npeak compute performance limits it to 400 GFLOP/s. The transition occurs\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I^*=P_{max}/B=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe original teaching diagram uses linear axes to display the bend\ndirectly; actual roofline analyses often use logarithmic axes.<\/p>\n<p>A kernel with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFLOP and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\cdot10^8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransferred bytes has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts computation takes at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^9/(100\\cdot10^9)=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns in this idealised model. Startup costs, synchronisation, transfer\nprotocols and dependent operations are not included.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>P<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">400<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> B<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">50<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>roof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> i:<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(P,B<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> [roof(i) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>]]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">400<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">400<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>work<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> moved<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>I<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>F(work,moved)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> lower<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>F(work,roof(I)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> lower<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(P<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>B,roof(I),<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(lower))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a bound to measurement<\/h2>\n<p>The model provides an upper bound on throughput, not a guaranteed\nmeasurement. Profiling records actual transfers, utilisation and\nruntime. A kernel operating far below the bound may suffer irregular\naccesses or inadequate vectorisation, among other problems. Sparse\nmatrices introduce index transfers alongside their values. A denser\nrepresentation may execute more operations and yet be faster on a\nparticular device; this must be measured. Strong scaling holds problem\nsize constant while adding compute units; weak scaling increases problem\nsize with the number of units. Communication and uneven data partitions\nlimit both. Optimisation must preserve the required numerical accuracy\nand reproducibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>If memory bandwidth doubles to 100 GB/s with unchanged peak\ncomputation, the transition shifts to 4 FLOP/byte. The kernel at\nintensity 2 can theoretically achieve up to 200 GFLOP/s; the kernel at\nintensity 12 remains limited to 400 GFLOP/s.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/nmds/00-Background/02-Performance.html\">Bindel:\nNumerical Methods for Data Science – Performance Basics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching\ntext consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://amcr.lbl.gov/departments/computer-science-department/ppan/roofline-performance-model/\">Berkeley\nLab: The Roofline Model: Visualizing and Optimizing Performance<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nspecialist page consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M27-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M27-L9 – AI-Assisted Software Development and Verifiable Coding Agents<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M27 – AI Systems Engineering and MLOps<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Optional specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, M14, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate agent-driven software\nchanges with restricted permissions, independent tests and reproducible\nevidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a code suggestion to a verified patch<\/h2>\n<p>A coding agent can break down a task, inspect files, propose changes\nand execute test programs. These actions are not equivalent: an\nexplanation is not a code change, a change is not a passing test, and a\npassing test does not demonstrate complete correctness. A verifiable\nprocess starts with clear requirements, a reproducible baseline and\nexpected behaviour. The agent works in an isolated working tree with\nminimal permissions. Network access, secrets, publishing and\nirreversible integration steps must be controlled separately. Files,\nissues and test outputs may contain misleading instructions; they are\ndata, not automatic authorisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A patch for an edge case<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001536_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Request leads to isolated patch and independent tests; failures return to patching, successes require diff review and approval; naive pass 3/6 versus verified pass 6/6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Request leads to isolated patch and\nindependent tests; failures return to patching, successes require diff\nreview and approval; naive pass 3/6 versus verified pass\n6/6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: From a change request to a verified patch.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A function computes the failure rate from counts of failed and total\nrequests. With no requests, it should return <code>None<\/code>, since a\nrate cannot be estimated. It should raise an error for negative counts\nor more failures than requests. A naïve patch,\n<code>bad / max(total, 1)<\/code>, avoids division by zero but\nincorrectly reports a zero rate when the dataset is empty. An\nindependent test reveals the semantic error. The diagram shows a\ndecision sequence that returns to the patch after a failed check. The\ncode compares two manually written candidates and simulates an agentic\nverification loop without invoking a language model.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> naive(bad,total):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> bad<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(total,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> checked(bad,total):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>bad<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>total:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;invalid counts&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> bad<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>total <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> total <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cases<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span>),(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> evaluate(fn):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    passed<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> bad,total,expected <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            observed<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>fn(bad,total)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            observed<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        passed.append(observed<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>expected)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> passed<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(evaluate(naive))<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(evaluate(checked))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(evaluate(naive),evaluate(checked))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evaluation and limitations of agentic work<\/h2>\n<p>Repository-based benchmarks such as SWE-bench assess whether systems\ncan resolve specific software issues through code changes. In a real\nworking environment, passing tests are only one concern: task scope,\npreserved existing functions, security and resource use also matter.\nAgents must not weaken tests merely to make a submission pass. Useful\nsafeguards include independent regression tests, diff review, static\nanalysis and negative test cases. Test output should identify the tested\nversion and be reproducible so a reviewer can rerun the patch. Even a\nfully green test run supports only the properties tested, not an absence\nof every possible defect. Security-critical changes or actions with\nexternal effects require explicit approval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>An agent removes the test for empty input and then reports success.\nThis is not a valid fix: the requirement is unmet and the measuring\nprocedure has been altered. Restore the independent tests, examine the\ndiff, and run both edge-case checks and existing regression tests on the\nversion actually proposed for integration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06770\">Jimenez et al. (2024):\nSWE-bench: Can Language Models Resolve Real-World GitHub Issues?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nabstract consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/\">Google SRE\nWorkbook: Canarying Releases<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 16 consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
